WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:02.719
Film Courage: Let’s go back to the writing
process with you and Melissa.

00:00:02.719 --> 00:00:09.910
So what was going on in your life when you
started coming up with ideas for ALL I WANT?

00:00:09.910 --> 00:00:13.130
I know it was her idea for the story initially,
right?

00:00:13.130 --> 00:00:14.660
West Liang: Yeah.

00:00:14.660 --> 00:00:20.630
I was in the middle of finishing a full-length
play and I was in the middle of workshopping

00:00:20.630 --> 00:00:29.240
that play because it was a story of what I
wanted to work out and was doing that.

00:00:29.240 --> 00:00:37.370
And so when she first approached me my mind
was really in another story in terms of my

00:00:37.370 --> 00:00:41.829
writing.

00:00:41.829 --> 00:00:47.320
And as an actor, I was taking myself out of
things a little bit because of whatever reason.

00:00:47.320 --> 00:00:55.950
I’d just gotten disenchanted with the process
of an actor’s life.

00:00:55.950 --> 00:01:03.640
So I started writing something to produce
and direct and when she approached me there

00:01:03.640 --> 00:01:08.039
was something about the story.

00:01:08.039 --> 00:01:13.909
Both Melissa and I are big fans of ensemble
movies and so that was our initial kind of

00:01:13.909 --> 00:01:17.859
connection, where we are fans of ensemble
films.

00:01:17.859 --> 00:01:24.579
And I also think that the theme of what we’re
trying to talk about in this movie is a really

00:01:24.579 --> 00:01:34.999
important theme for me and for her this idea
of people in the modern American city right

00:01:34.999 --> 00:01:40.979
now (I feel like, at least certainly in Los
Angeles) there is kind of this undercurrent

00:01:40.979 --> 00:01:46.249
of stress and anxiety and depression that
people don’t talk about.

00:01:46.249 --> 00:01:47.710
Film Courage: You see it, though.

00:01:47.710 --> 00:01:48.710
You feel it.

00:01:48.710 --> 00:01:49.850
West Liang: You feel it.

00:01:49.850 --> 00:01:51.450
You bump up against it.

00:01:51.450 --> 00:01:57.689
And I’m not talking about clinical disorders
of anxiety or depression or things like that.

00:01:57.689 --> 00:02:10.869
I’m talking about the garden variety kind
of weight of anxiety, of stress, of depression.

00:02:10.869 --> 00:02:22.860
And that is for me feels like a very un-talked
about area of modern life.

00:02:22.860 --> 00:02:29.040
People who are trying to figure out - what
kind of jobs they want to have?

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:30.099
Do they want to switch careers?

00:02:30.099 --> 00:02:32.680
Do they want to get married and have kids?

00:02:32.680 --> 00:02:35.769
Are they happy with their partner?

00:02:35.769 --> 00:02:38.970
With their families?

00:02:38.970 --> 00:02:44.160
And I think thematically that was something
that we both wanted kind of pull apart and

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:45.160
examine.

00:02:45.160 --> 00:02:51.870
That’s the wonderful thing about this film
is that it’s got amazing performances across

00:02:51.870 --> 00:02:53.150
the board.

00:02:53.150 --> 00:03:01.120
There is amazing technical prowess in terms
of cinematography and editing and composition.

00:03:01.120 --> 00:03:07.189
I’m certainly proud of my work as a director,
Melissa and I are very proud of it as producing

00:03:07.189 --> 00:03:16.219
partners and someone who collaborated to make
the script happen but thematically I think

00:03:16.219 --> 00:03:20.010
it’s an interesting thing to talk about
for the audience that we don’t already talk

00:03:20.010 --> 00:03:21.010
about right now.

00:03:21.010 --> 00:03:28.030
Film Courage: I know it’s taboo because
nobody wants to get too heavy, but I sense

00:03:28.030 --> 00:03:32.030
it living in LA as long as I have, I’ve
seen the change and I don’t know if it’s

00:03:32.030 --> 00:03:33.030
because it’s busier here.

00:03:33.030 --> 00:03:37.150
And on a side note (and we can take this out
if you don’t want to talk about it) but

00:03:37.150 --> 00:03:39.719
where you live (and I won’t say where it
is), but it’s removed…

00:03:39.719 --> 00:03:41.189
West Liang: In the Hollywood Hills (smiling).

00:03:41.189 --> 00:03:45.040
Film Courage: Well it’s removed from Hollywood
and you have a lovely place here by the way.

00:03:45.040 --> 00:03:50.420
And I can see why you would want to be here
because it’s not part of the industry but

00:03:50.420 --> 00:03:55.719
you are so creative and you do produce things,
you don’t just talk about them (you have

00:03:55.719 --> 00:03:57.290
a body of work).

00:03:57.290 --> 00:04:00.709
Why choose some place that’s not in the
heart of the industry?

00:04:00.709 --> 00:04:07.930
West Liang: Well it wasn’t initially by
choice, it was based on so many different

00:04:07.930 --> 00:04:13.519
factors in terms of where you want to put
yourself, where do you want to make your nest

00:04:13.519 --> 00:04:19.940
per se oftentimes when you don’t have all
the resources you want to have.

00:04:19.940 --> 00:04:26.130
But I think as a result of all these different
factors forcing me to make a decision I’m

00:04:26.130 --> 00:04:37.930
able to kind of have a home that feels a little
bit, kind of on the outskirts of the hub of

00:04:37.930 --> 00:04:40.330
Hollywood.

00:04:40.330 --> 00:04:47.210
And I think that gives me a certain sense
of I don’t know whether it’s distance

00:04:47.210 --> 00:04:58.990
or peace from it or whatever…having some
quiet time to work on whatever you want to

00:04:58.990 --> 00:05:00.990
work on.

00:05:00.990 --> 00:05:06.470
Film Courage: So you don’t feel that pull
to be in that sort of The Hub?

00:05:06.470 --> 00:05:08.669
West Liang: No, no, no.

00:05:08.669 --> 00:05:09.669
I mean not anymore.

00:05:09.669 --> 00:05:14.860
I think when I first moved down to LA it was
really important for me to be surrounded by

00:05:14.860 --> 00:05:19.621
where everything is happening and that was
exciting for many years and I think now it’s

00:05:19.621 --> 00:05:28.160
kind of nice to sort of be around that, have
access to that, but also have the ability

00:05:28.160 --> 00:05:33.199
to tuck yourself away and not be surrounded
by that.

00:05:33.199 --> 00:05:36.410
Film Courage: Are you more creative in the
daytime or nighttime?

00:05:36.410 --> 00:05:40.849
West Liang: Definitely in the day time, yeah
I think so.

00:05:40.849 --> 00:05:44.039
I am more of a morning person.

00:05:44.039 --> 00:05:48.761
And I don’t know if Melissa has ever complained
about it?

00:05:48.761 --> 00:05:51.860
I don’t think so but every time we would
have meetings I would always be like “Let’s

00:05:51.860 --> 00:05:54.320
do it at 8:00 in the morning.”

00:05:54.320 --> 00:05:56.629
And she was like “Yeah, sure.”

00:05:56.629 --> 00:05:58.669
I think that’s also kind of interesting,
too.

00:05:58.669 --> 00:06:04.159
Because as a side note I’m collaborating
on another project with someone else who is

00:06:04.159 --> 00:06:08.110
a night person.

00:06:08.110 --> 00:06:12.009
In some interesting ways, that’s kind of
caused some friction.

00:06:12.009 --> 00:06:20.440
Because after 8:00 p.m. my mind just relaxes
and starts to think about other things.

00:06:20.440 --> 00:06:26.360
And I kind of want to eat and I kind of want
to watch a movie, I want to just go to the

00:06:26.360 --> 00:06:30.639
gym and do nothing.

00:06:30.639 --> 00:06:37.050
And during the day I’m really productive
and for this other person, it’s the opposite.

00:06:37.050 --> 00:06:39.000
Film Courage: Interesting.

00:06:39.000 --> 00:06:46.771
West Liang: It would be interesting to take
a tally to see where are, are they a morning

00:06:46.771 --> 00:06:52.199
or a night person and also their collaborators
are they the same or opposite?

00:06:52.199 --> 00:06:53.199
What are you?

00:06:53.199 --> 00:06:56.500
Are you a morning creative person or?

00:06:56.500 --> 00:07:01.111
Film Courage: I would say I get much more
done after a cup of coffee and in the morning

00:07:01.111 --> 00:07:08.210
and then past a certain point I’m just done
and I don’t…nothing is worse than being

00:07:08.210 --> 00:07:14.319
behind a computer when it’s like 2:00 a.m.
and I can’t see straight and that’s not

00:07:14.319 --> 00:07:16.540
where my best self lies.

00:07:16.540 --> 00:07:18.720
So I get that.

00:07:18.720 --> 00:07:21.860
But I know there are some people that come
alive at night.

00:07:21.860 --> 00:07:25.300
I think we’re all just hardwired differently
and there is no right way.

00:07:25.300 --> 00:07:28.960
I mean you hear of these entrepreneurs that
get up at like 4:45 a.m. and they take a cold

00:07:28.960 --> 00:07:33.069
shower and they’re ready and they’re doing
a wheatgrass smoothie.

00:07:33.069 --> 00:07:34.069
And that’s great.

00:07:34.069 --> 00:07:36.220
I don’t think I can do 4:45 a.m.

00:07:36.220 --> 00:07:38.130
Everyone has their own process.

00:07:38.130 --> 00:07:42.330
But then there are some people who fool themselves
and that’s the trick I worry about.

00:07:42.330 --> 00:07:52.509
West Liang: I definitely think that the footnote
to that question is really like when do your

00:07:52.509 --> 00:08:00.340
inspirations come and for me they come all
over the place and at any time but oddly it

00:08:00.340 --> 00:08:05.889
comes at night often as well.

00:08:05.889 --> 00:08:10.280
I just think that you let it sit.

00:08:10.280 --> 00:08:17.550
Inspiration comes to you, ideas come to you
and you let it sit around in your head and

00:08:17.550 --> 00:08:22.919
you can’t really actively do anything about
it, I just sort of let it sit and then I will

00:08:22.919 --> 00:08:26.419
try to actually execute on the idea during
the day.

00:08:26.419 --> 00:08:30.100
Film Courage: Did you grow up in a big city?

00:08:30.100 --> 00:08:35.320
West Liang: I grew up in the suburbs of the
San Francisco Bay Area.

00:08:35.320 --> 00:08:40.980
I mean I worked and played in The City but
suburbia.

00:08:40.980 --> 00:08:53.700
LA is unique in some ways in a sense that
there are so many ambitious people coming

00:08:53.700 --> 00:09:01.050
here to do what seems to me like kind of an
impossible kind of task of making your living

00:09:01.050 --> 00:09:03.200
as an artist.

00:09:03.200 --> 00:09:15.460
And so I think maybe with that, maybe where
the country is or expectations or all these

00:09:15.460 --> 00:09:21.190
kind of different things cause anxiety in
the city.

00:09:21.190 --> 00:09:31.660
Cause anxiety in people who are expected to
get married or expected to make a certain

00:09:31.660 --> 00:09:39.700
amount of money in their jobs or expected
to do certain things and how that bumps up

00:09:39.700 --> 00:09:43.250
against what you are able to do.

00:09:43.250 --> 00:09:49.010
Film Courage: I think the money part is a
huge factor in this city.

00:09:49.010 --> 00:09:59.120
West Liang: Yeah…being able to afford where
to live.

00:09:59.120 --> 00:10:08.580
It’s tough which is why for me I wanted
to really explore that in this movie.

00:10:08.580 --> 00:10:13.040
This movie is very much about friendship,
it’s about family, love, modern love and

00:10:13.040 --> 00:10:15.380
life and all those kind of things.

00:10:15.380 --> 00:10:20.730
There’s a lot of humor in this story, there’s
a lot of fun moments in the story that I think

00:10:20.730 --> 00:10:22.710
the audience can really enjoy.

00:10:22.710 --> 00:10:27.690
They can see themselves and their friends
and their family in their characters in this

00:10:27.690 --> 00:10:38.910
movie but I think at the same time it also
starts conversation about that, about anxiety.

